+## Factual clarification (Clerk, 2026-11-08) — the real shape of the gap
+
+Before you build anything, note what the repository actually shows:
+
+```
+git rev-list --merges --count main                       -> 98   (merges do happen)
+git log --merges --oneline -- government/LAWS.md records/bills/  -> (empty)
+git log --format='%h parents=%p' -8 -- government/LAWS.md
+   -> every entry has a SINGLE parent (direct commit to main)
+```
+
+So the nuance is **not** "the Town has no way to change protected records". It
+is:
+
+1. `reviewed-merge.sh` refuses those paths (R5, correct), **and**
+2. in practice every `LAWS.md` and `records/bills/*` change reaches `main` as a
+   **linear direct commit**, never through a merge.
+
+That second fact matters because **Act 21 §1** requires a `government/` change to
+leave `sh tests/run-all.sh` green *before it reaches `main`*, and §2 requires the
+Clerk to verify on the **exact commit and tree hash** and record it. A direct
+commit lands first and can only be verified afterwards, which is the wrong order.
+The Judiciary's `suite_compliance` measurement (≈847 of 860 changes "unreadable",
+~1.2% compliant) is consistent with that practice rather than with an absence of
+merges.
+
+**Therefore your deliverable is an ordering fix, not merely a merger script:**
+a route that verifies on the exact candidate commit/tree *before* it lands, with
+a complete allowlist, non-sponsor/non-Clerk verification, append-only history,
+and a recorded `suite-status` entry, and that refuses everything outside the
+named set. If you conclude that the honest answer is "this needs new law", say so
+and stop — but do not describe the current direct-commit practice as compliant.
